# rlxfw's declared build stamp.   P4a, 2026-09-01.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, and it is a defect report rather than a preference.
# 量 2026-09-01: two builds of the SAME tree, from the SAME .config file, the
# same initramfs spec and the same marks, run back to back (讀
# `r3-9/determinism.log`: cells starting 40 s apart, whose kernel stamps end up
# 48 s apart and whose cpio stamps 33 s), produce different `vmlinux` sha256.  84 of 3,935,472 bytes differ; ZERO of them are in `.text`.
# Six are the kernel's build timestamp -- `linux_banner` in `.rodata` and the
# `version` field of `init_uts_ns` in `.data`, which are two copies of the one
# `UTS_VERSION` string that `scripts/mkcompile_h` writes from `date` when
# KBUILD_BUILD_TIMESTAMP is unset (讀, mkcompile_h:38).  The other 78 are
# `usr/gen_init_cpio`'s clock; `host-compat/0002` is that half.
#
# WHAT THE VALUE IS.  A Unix epoch, decimal, one line.  Everything else is a
# comment.  `tools/rlxfw-kbuild.sh` turns it into both of the things a build
# needs from it, so they cannot drift apart:
#
#     KBUILD_BUILD_TIMESTAMP   LC_ALL=C TZ=UTC date -u -d @<epoch>
#     RLXFW_CPIO_MTIME         <epoch>
#
# LC_ALL and TZ are pinned in the driver rather than inherited.  A stamp that
# reads `CST` on this machine and `UTC` on another is not a declaration, and
# the whole reason this file exists is that a build input living outside a
# committed file is a build nobody can check.
#
# WHY MIDNIGHT UTC, AND WHY THAT IS NOT LAZINESS.  The value below renders as
#
#     Tue Sep  1 00:00:00 UTC 2026
#
# A reader who sees exactly 00:00:00 UTC in a banner knows immediately that it
# is DECLARED and not a build moment.  A stamp that looks like a plausible
# working hour would read as a measurement, and this project's rule is that a
# number which reads as a measurement and is not one is worse than no number.
#
# 🔴 WHAT FREEZING THIS COSTS, because it is not free.  `PROGRESS.md`'s
# anti-DoD names three strings the vendor image cannot produce, and one of them
# is this build stamp: `(key@K) ... #1 Fri Aug 28 23:37:47 CST 2026`.  Freezing
# the time half does NOT delete that leg -- `(key@K)` is inside it and no
# vendor image carries it -- but it removes the leg's second role: telling
# WHICH of my builds is running, from the console, on the board.  That role
# moves to `ID0` in `config/rlxfw-marks.tsv`, which is a board-side string
# derived from the recipe rather than from the clock.  Nothing else in the
# image distinguished one of my builds from another: `RLXFW-B00`..`B10` are
# constants and `start address: 0x80003600` is a property of the link.
#
# WHEN TO CHANGE IT.  Deliberately, in a commit that says why.  ⚠️ Two
# different source trees stamped with the same value are NOT distinguished by
# the banner -- that is what `ID0` is for, and `ID0` needs no remembering
# because it is computed from the declaration files themselves.
#
# HOW TO BUILD WITHOUT IT.  `rlxfw-kbuild.sh --no-stamp`, which is deliberate,
# says so in its name, and prints that the build is not reproducible.  A stamp
# file that declares no epoch is REFUSED rather than silently treated as
# --no-stamp: "use the clock" has to be asked for, never arrived at.  That is
# the shape `config/rlxfw-cflags` already uses and the reason is the same.

